WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
How do you trace one neuron inside a tangled slice of brain?

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.860
Stain every cell, and you get a dense crowd of bodies wrapped in threads.

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.060
No eye can follow a single branch from start to end.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.320
In 1873, a surprising stain turned that puzzle around.

5
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:28.030
A Nissl stain binds RNA-rich cytoplasm around every nucleus, marking cell bodies and

6
00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:32.530
layer boundaries. Golgi's silver chromate stain does the opposite.

7
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.890
For reasons nobody fully understands, it blackens only a small, unpredictable

8
00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.070
fraction of cells.

9
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:46.510
Yet each stained neuron fills almost completely, revealing every dendrite and fibre

10
00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:47.770
against a clear background.

11
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:51.813
Seeing whole neurons sharpened an old question.

12
00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:58.653
Is the nervous system separate cells, like liver or skin, or one continuous network

13
00:00:58.653 --> 00:01:04.513
of fused fibres? Zoom into this same stained slice where two blackened branches

14
00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:10.733
meet. Can Golgi's stain under a light microscope prove whether those two cells are

15
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:11.753
separate or fused?

16
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.707
No. Light blurs tiny gaps.

17
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:26.987
The best light microscope blurs points closer than about 200 nanometres side to

18
00:01:26.987 --> 00:01:33.607
side. At a chemical synapse, where one cell signals the next, the gap is about 20

19
00:01:33.607 --> 00:01:40.287
nanometres. 200 divided by 20 is 10, so the gap sits ten times below the limit.

20
00:01:40.287 --> 00:01:44.187
Where branches met, Golgi and Cajal saw no gap.

21
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:47.313
Two more limits hide in the same slice.

22
00:01:48.553 --> 00:01:52.293
A stained section is 100 micrometres or more thick.

23
00:01:53.593 --> 00:01:59.353
Focusing up and down blurs roughly 0.5 to 1 micrometre, so stacked branches look

24
00:01:59.353 --> 00:02:04.113
joined. Every Golgi specimen is also dead, fixed tissue.

25
00:02:05.373 --> 00:02:10.153
A still picture shows shape and position, never movement and never a cell's

26
00:02:10.153 --> 00:02:11.153
descendants.

27
00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:15.383
Experimenters guard against these traps by pairing methods.

28
00:02:16.623 --> 00:02:22.163
Because Golgi fills an unpredictable fraction, Nissl stains handle cell counts and

29
00:02:22.163 --> 00:02:29.343
layer boundaries. Immunostaining uses an antibody against one chosen protein to test

30
00:02:29.343 --> 00:02:31.243
whether that target was detected.

31
00:02:32.743 --> 00:02:37.703
Electron microscopy resolves true gaps, while proving movement takes more than extra

32
00:02:37.703 --> 00:02:38.703
fixed sections.

33
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:44.293
Used within those limits, Golgi's stain unlocked foundational discoveries.

34
00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:52.753
Its 1873 arrival let Golgi and Cajal argue separate cells versus fused fibres with

35
00:02:52.753 --> 00:03:00.193
evidence. In 1890, Santiago Ramón y Cajal stained chick embryo spinal cords a few

36
00:03:00.193 --> 00:03:06.153
days old. He found young nerve fibres ending in a swollen club with spikes, and

37
00:03:06.153 --> 00:03:07.193
named it the growth cone.

38
00:03:08.317 --> 00:03:15.777
In 1972, Pasko Rakic paired Golgi stains with serial electron microscopy in fetal

39
00:03:15.777 --> 00:03:17.117
rhesus monkey cortex.

40
00:03:18.457 --> 00:03:22.957
He found young neurons pressed against radial fibres on their way to the cortical

41
00:03:22.957 --> 00:03:27.397
plate. Pairing methods answered what light alone could not.

42
00:03:28.657 --> 00:03:33.677
Leaving most cells unseen reveals whole shapes, once paired with tools for gaps and

43
00:03:33.677 --> 00:03:33.997
motion.
